# TODO - Safe automatic application updates

## Objective

Offer opt-in public GitHub updates with trusted compatible artifacts, safe
staging, visible status, and recoverable installation. Keep startup usable
offline and preserve teacher data and current access boundaries.

## Acceptance

### Independent preferences and ownership

Expose separate EN/ES controls: “Start Blooket API at login” / “Iniciar
Blooket API al iniciar sesión” and “Check for and install updates
automatically” / “Buscar e instalar actualizaciones automáticamente”. Both
default false; a missing update preference migrates to false.

Task `blooket-13` alone owns native login registration and service lifecycle.
This record owns update integration and acceptance, reusing that lifecycle
rather than installing another startup agent. Never silently enable persistence
or background network activity.

Show current version, last check, and update state, plus “Check for updates” /
“Buscar actualizaciones”. Manual checks work with automation off; manual
download/installation requires explicit local action. Disabling automation stops
future scheduled checks/downloads and cancels uncommitted background work.

Checks and installation remain local product behavior, with no remote MCP shell,
arbitrary-download, or installation tools and no broadened OAuth permissions.

### Public source and bounded checks

Use this repository's public GitHub Releases REST API without a teacher GitHub
account or repository credential; never scrape rendered HTML:
`https://api.github.com/repos/albertovillaosorno/blooket-api/releases`.

Bound metadata to ten seconds, one megabyte, and at most two pages of 50
releases; admit pagination only for this exact release endpoint. A truncated
catalog is unavailable, not proof of being current. Preserve normal HTTPS
validation and bound download size, redirects, and deadlines separately.

When enabled, check at least 30 seconds after usable startup and at most once
per 24 hours. Coalesce manual/concurrent checks, honor server rate/retry limits,
and back off at one, six, then 24 hours. Sleep/offline/reconnect cannot cause a
burst; an ETag may support conditional requests without tracking identifiers.

Network failures never crash the app or block offline startup. Do not transmit
Blooket/MCP/tunnel credentials, teacher identifiers, lessons, or media metadata
to GitHub; update checks are not telemetry.

### Results and release selection

Distinguish current/latest, newer compatible version, downloading, ready,
installation pending safe restart, applied, source unavailable, malformed or
untrusted metadata, no compatible asset, download failure,
integrity/authenticity failure, installation failure, and rollback/recovery.

A valid empty catalog or no newer admitted release says “No new version is
available” / “No hay una nueva versión disponible”. HTTP/DNS errors, timeout,
invalid JSON/schema, and truncation show a bounded useful failure instead.

Admit only final public releases, excluding drafts and prereleases. Use the same
canonical `YY.Q.PATCH` parser as the release pipeline: tag `v26.4.0`, product
`26.4.0`. Order year, quarter, and revision numerically; ignore older and equal
versions and choose the highest admitted version rather than list order/title.

Reject future UTC calendar quarters and publication times more than five minutes
ahead of the current clock. Reject malformed objects; skip malformed tags with
bounded diagnostics, but report untrusted metadata if all candidate tags are
malformed. A higher patch must remain monotonic within its quarter.

Select exactly one `darwin-arm64.zip` on the supported ARM64 Mac target. The
archive must contain exactly the expected `Blooket API.app` bundle identity;
missing, duplicate, foreign, or incompatible assets have explicit outcomes.
Linux is a development package and is never an automatic-update target.

### Trust and release pipeline

The current pipeline publishes no update manifest or final archive checksums.
Dependency hashes in `distribution.json` do not verify a downloaded app archive.
Add a bounded CI-owned manifest with canonical version/tag, source commit,
target, minimum OS, asset name, byte size, and SHA-256 after
signing/notarization. Validate it against tested archives and publish only those
exact artifacts after the release gate and CI succeed.

Require publisher authenticity through a signed manifest with an admitted key or
verified CI provenance, with specified trust-key rotation. Preserve Apple
signing, designated requirement/Team ID, Gatekeeper, and notarization checks.
Checksums alone do not authenticate the publisher; HTTPS alone does not trust a
ZIP. Do not weaken signing or bypass legitimate OS authorization.

Validate asset URLs and bounded redirects against the exact repository, tag, and
expected GitHub release asset authority. Reject foreign URLs, extraction path
traversal, unsafe symlinks, and mismatched bundle identity/version/target.

### Transaction and recovery

Download automatically only when opted in, or after explicit local action. Stage
safely and apply at a safe lifecycle boundary; never blindly overwrite a running
application. Wait for active quiz mutations and block installation on ambiguous
journals until reconciliation or a human checkpoint; restart must not replay a
remote write.

Reuse service stop/start ownership and the single-instance lock. Journal phases
and use atomic replacement or a recoverable staged transaction. Retain or
recover the old usable app on failure; clean only owned abandoned downloads and
show sanitized recoverable errors.

Separate the application bundle from user data. Preserve media, metadata,
preferences, skills, drafts, credential references, and Blooket/tunnel settings.
Schema migrations are versioned, fail closed, and retain a recoverable original.
Do not silently reset configuration.

OAuth tokens are currently memory-bound and renewed consent may be necessary
after restart; show that authorization boundary without claiming token survival
or resetting saved preferences. Required macOS replacement approval is a real
human boundary, never a bypass target.

### Acceptance matrix

Use deterministic fixtures/doubles without live GitHub polling for no releases,
current/newer/older/future versions, numeric ordering, drafts/prereleases,
malformed tags/metadata/JSON, HTTP/DNS/timeouts, size/pagination limits,
compatible/incompatible/duplicate/foreign assets, integrity/authenticity
failure, interrupted downloads/staging/install, recovery/rollback, settings
off/on, manual checks, backoff/sleep/reconnect, active/ambiguous writes, and
data retention through failed migrations.

Preserve launch-at-login preference independently across updates, remove only
owned persistence on uninstall, and recover visible registration/start/removal
failures. Platform-only native acceptance belongs to task blooket-15.

Before completion, exercise the production updater contract against a signed
ARM64 release fixture through staging, bundle verification, replacement,
restart, rollback/data retention, and EN/ES states. Platform-only Apple trust
acceptance belongs to task blooket-15; this task authorizes no release/tag.

## Evidence

The launcher already owns service startup and duplicate-instance protection.
`launchAtLogin` defaults false, but native registration remains unimplemented.
Manual public catalog checking is implemented; automatic installation and its
preference remain pending.

The public [release page][releases] had no releases when inspected without
authentication on 2026-10-06. An empty catalog is a valid state. Protocol
references are GitHub's [release API][api] and [asset API][assets].

### Portable catalog checking

The local Configuration page shows product version, last-check time, a manual
check button, and distinct EN/ES current, available, unsupported-host,
incompatible-asset, source-failure, and invalid-metadata states. No startup
check, automatic polling, download, installer, or remote MCP tool is enabled.

The IR decoder and selector enforce the fixed repository, canonical product
versions, final-release selection, numeric ordering, UTC future rejection,
actual host architecture, unique assets, and `verificationRequired: true`.
The anonymous HTTPS adapter bounds the entire request to ten seconds, two
50-entry pages, and one megabyte total; redirects and foreign pagination fail
closed. Abort cancels both request/body handling and a hanging injected port.

The application controller coalesces concurrent manual checks, caches a check
for at least thirty seconds, and honors admitted server retry intervals. Reads
of bootstrap/status stay offline. The local POST requires exact empty input,
same origin, and CSRF; remote callers cannot select URLs, target, or version.
Closing the service cancels its outstanding check without reporting success.

Deterministic portable tests cover metadata, asset authority, pagination,
timeouts, active cancellation, exact local input, CSRF, coalescing, retry
intervals, and offline startup. On 2026-10-07 an anonymous real API check
returned a `current` result. This is protocol evidence, not an
installation or published-artifact acceptance.

An isolated local page in Chrome was exercised with deterministic current,
timeout, and available fixtures. EN/ES messages, last-check time, and the
thirty-second button cooldown were observed without using teacher credentials
or modifying the running developer service. Fixture Mac targets are not native
Mac evidence.

Automatic preferences, scheduling/backoff, trusted manifests, verification,
staging, installation/recovery, and bundle replacement remain open. Platform
acceptance belongs to task blooket-15.
Do not mark this record completed based on the manual checker.
The page hides unsupported update controls when an older running service
omits update status; this compatibility state was observed in isolated Chrome
without restarting the active development service.

Validation on 2026-10-07 passed strict TypeScript, roadmap integrity, and the
full portable suite (671 tests at that snapshot). Jig reported only the known
external `JIG-RULE-GAP-001`; no local validation finding remained. Linux
package assembly and extracted verification passed on fixed commit `7472a8d`
in an isolated checkout; moving shared HEAD during a prior run caused its
revision check to fail and was not treated as package acceptance.

### Manifest verification foundation

Schema-one manifests now admit only this repository, canonical version/tag,
source commit, owned `Blooket API.app` identity, macOS minimum at least 13.5,
and exactly one ARM64 ZIP asset with byte size, canonical URL, and SHA-256.
Unknown fields, conflicting versions, duplicate/foreign assets, and invalid
hashes are rejected before signature verification.

The envelope admits Ed25519 only. Its key ID is the SHA-256 of an already
admitted local public key's SPKI DER; remote metadata cannot supply a key.
Signing bytes use `blooket-api:update-manifest:v1` followed by a newline and
canonical manifest JSON with fixed field/target order. Canonical base64url
signatures bind every field and the ARM64 archive hash to
the publisher key.

The portable verifier requires the signed version, tag, architecture, URL,
name, and byte size to match the selected public release. Byte verification
then checks actual length and SHA-256. Successful signature verification still
returns `requiresAppleVerification: true`; hashes and Ed25519 do not prove
Gatekeeper/notarization, bundle identity, minimum OS, or safe replacement.

Nine deterministic tests exercise canonicalization, exact contracts, signed
field/context tampering, wrong/retired keys, local overlapping key rotation,
release mismatches, changed bytes, and interrupted bytes. Ephemeral fixture
keys are not production trust roots and no signing secret was configured.
Strict TypeScript and the full portable suite passed (690 tests at that
snapshot); Jig retains the known external evidence gap.

A fixed checkout at `0f7eccd` subsequently passed strict TypeScript, roadmap
integrity, all 692 portable tests, and Linux package assembly/extracted checks.
The shared checkout has only the pre-existing external Jig evidence gap after
the other agent committed the test-format correction.

These helpers feed the staging adapter below, outside product UI/MCP authority.
CI still must publish the signed envelope after signing/notarization and
validation,
and the installed application must pin its real publisher keys with a governed
rotation policy. No production manifest, trust root, native installer, or
published-artifact acceptance is claimed.

### Signed archive staging foundation

The portable `update-downloads` adapter authenticates signed metadata against
the selected release and local public keys before disk or network activity.
It streams only the signed initial GitHub asset URL, with anonymous requests,
normal HTTPS validation, at most three redirects, and a two-minute overall
network deadline. Redirects admit only the exact initial URL or
`release-assets.githubusercontent.com`, the [documented release CDN][cdn].

Actual streamed bytes must match the signed size and SHA-256 before the owned
partial file is synced and renamed. Private generated staging directories and
files use owner-only permissions; no archive is extracted or application
replaced. Success still requires independent Apple verification and is never
reported as an installed or update-ready application.

A shared download lock refuses another live owner. Cancellation, timeouts,
HTTP failures, forbidden redirects, incomplete/oversized bytes, and hash errors
have bounded distinct results and remove owned partials. Cleanup failures are
reported explicitly, and lock release is attempted even if partial cleanup
fails; retained successful downloads still need governed lifecycle cleanup.

Deterministic tests cover the ARM64 target, streaming fidelity, private
permissions, unknown publishers, signed size/hash failures, admitted/foreign
redirects, redirect loops, hanging fetch/body handling, cancellation, writer
ownership, and HTTP failures. They use ephemeral signing keys and local doubles,
with no GitHub traffic, production keys, or downloaded Mac release acceptance.

Strict TypeScript, roadmap integrity, and all 741 portable tests passed at this
checkpoint. Jig retains only the known external evidence gap.

This is an adapter foundation, not an enabled product updater. Production key
provisioning, signed-manifest fetching/publication, opt-in scheduling and UI,
Apple validation, extraction/installation/recovery, and real release acceptance
remain pending.

## Blockers

Lifecycle safety, production publisher keys/manifest publication, extraction,
`Blooket API.app` identity verification, atomic replacement, restart, rollback,
and recovery remain functional work. Platform-only signing/notarization and
native acceptance belong to task blooket-15.

